1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,632
By the end of this video, you will know what every common speed skating gear word means.

2
00:00:05,632 --> 00:00:09,344
You will also know the order to build your first setup in.

3
00:00:09,344 --> 00:00:12,800
Frame, durometer, bore, ABEC, spacer, Hi Lo, triskate.

4
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:16,832
When you are new, these words fly past faster than the skaters.

5
00:00:16,832 --> 00:00:21,312
It can feel like everyone else got a glossary that you never received.

6
00:00:21,312 --> 00:00:23,425
So here it is, in plain language.

7
00:00:23,425 --> 00:00:30,977
Once the words make sense, you can read a product page, follow your coach, and make clear choices instead of guessing.

8
00:00:31,677 --> 00:00:34,092
Everything starts with your platform.

9
00:00:34,092 --> 00:00:38,335
That is the boot on your foot and the frame bolted underneath it.

10
00:00:38,335 --> 00:00:40,619
The boot holds your foot and ankle.

11
00:00:40,619 --> 00:00:46,298
The frame is the stiff beam that carries your wheels in a straight line under the boot.

12
00:00:46,298 --> 00:00:49,627
On a speed skate, the frame is long, low and rigid.

13
00:00:49,627 --> 00:00:50,280
Why rigid?

14
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:56,090
Because any flex in the frame is energy you push into the frame instead of into the road.

15
00:00:56,090 --> 00:01:02,813
A good frame keeps your wheels perfectly in line, so the skate rolls true and does not scrub off speed.

16
00:01:03,513 --> 00:01:05,938
Speed frames come in two families.

17
00:01:05,938 --> 00:01:11,359
A four wheel frame carries four smaller wheels, often 90 or 100 millimetres.

18
00:01:11,359 --> 00:01:19,633
It sits lower with a longer contact patch, and many skaters find it stable and forgiving while they build technique.

19
00:01:19,633 --> 00:01:27,123
A three wheel frame, also called a triskate, carries three larger wheels, such as 110 or 125 millimetres.

20
00:01:27,123 --> 00:01:32,686
It sits taller, rolls faster once you are moving, and is lighter for its size.

21
00:01:32,686 --> 00:01:33,756
Two more words.

22
00:01:33,756 --> 00:01:39,035
Platform length is the distance between the front and rear mounting bolts.

23
00:01:39,035 --> 00:01:42,815
Longer tracks straighter, shorter turns more eagerly.

24
00:01:42,815 --> 00:01:51,802
Hi Lo means the front wheels are smaller than the rear ones, which lowers your toe and helps you sit forward into your stroke.

25
00:01:51,802 --> 00:01:55,083
You do not need to fine tune these on day one.

26
00:01:55,783 --> 00:01:58,106
Here is the wheel size ladder.

27
00:01:58,106 --> 00:02:01,745
On the left, 90 millimetres, nimble and stable.

28
00:02:01,745 --> 00:02:04,996
Next, 100 millimetres, the all round pick.

29
00:02:04,996 --> 00:02:09,100
Then 110 millimetres, fast and popular for marathons.

30
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:13,745
And on the right, 125 millimetres, built for top speed roll.

31
00:02:13,745 --> 00:02:15,216
The rule is simple.

32
00:02:15,216 --> 00:02:18,467
Bigger wheels roll faster and carry speed.

33
00:02:18,467 --> 00:02:23,113
Smaller wheels feel more stable and get moving more quickly.

34
00:02:23,113 --> 00:02:28,145
Most beginners are well served in the 90 to 100 millimetre range.

35
00:02:28,845 --> 00:02:31,472
The second wheel number is durometer.

36
00:02:31,472 --> 00:02:38,148
It tells you how hard or soft the wheel is, written as a number and the letter A, such as 85A.

37
00:02:38,148 --> 00:02:40,775
A higher number means a harder wheel.

38
00:02:40,775 --> 00:02:45,462
Harder wheels roll faster on smooth, hot surfaces and last longer.

39
00:02:45,462 --> 00:02:51,143
Softer wheels grip more and soak up rough ground, at the cost of a little speed.

40
00:02:51,143 --> 00:02:53,771
Many race wheels use a label instead.

41
00:02:53,771 --> 00:03:02,221
XFirm is the softer one, with more grip, which helps on a slick or dusty track, or while you learn to trust your edges.

42
00:03:02,221 --> 00:03:06,198
XXFirm is harder and rolls faster on smooth, hot tarmac.

43
00:03:06,198 --> 00:03:11,169
Pick for your surface and your confidence, not just the fastest label.

44
00:03:11,869 --> 00:03:17,567
Bearings are the small steel or ceramic rings that let each wheel spin freely on its axle.

45
00:03:17,567 --> 00:03:18,959
Every wheel needs two.

46
00:03:18,959 --> 00:03:25,227
So a pair of four wheel skates runs sixteen bearings, and a pair of three wheel skates runs twelve.

47
00:03:25,227 --> 00:03:31,431
Bearings are usually sold in sets of sixteen for this reason, so check the count before you order.

48
00:03:31,431 --> 00:03:35,736
They are the cheapest way to feel a real difference in your skating.

49
00:03:35,736 --> 00:03:41,877
They are also the easiest part to ruin through neglect, so they are worth understanding properly.

50
00:03:42,577 --> 00:03:49,036
Bore is the size of the hole in the middle of the bearing, where the axle passes through.

51
00:03:49,036 --> 00:03:54,406
On the left is a 608, the inline speed bearing, with an 8 millimetre bore.

52
00:03:54,406 --> 00:03:59,777
On the right is a 627, used on many quad skates, with a 7 millimetre bore.

53
00:03:59,777 --> 00:04:02,970
It will not fit an 8 millimetre inline axle.

54
00:04:02,970 --> 00:04:07,542
The two look almost identical in a photo, but they do not swap.

55
00:04:07,542 --> 00:04:11,606
This is the most common ordering mistake beginners make.

56
00:04:11,606 --> 00:04:15,453
So always match the bore to your axle before you buy.

57
00:04:16,153 --> 00:04:21,838
ABEC is a manufacturing tolerance rating, with odd numbers like 1, 3, 5, 7 and 9.

58
00:04:21,838 --> 00:04:24,645
A higher number means tighter machining.

59
00:04:24,645 --> 00:04:31,523
But it does not measure speed by itself, and for skating it is only one small part of the picture.

60
00:04:31,523 --> 00:04:38,892
The quality of the balls, races and cage matters far more, and so does how clean and oiled you keep them.

61
00:04:38,892 --> 00:04:42,401
Steel balls are proven, tough and excellent value.

62
00:04:42,401 --> 00:04:48,648
Ceramic balls are lighter, harder, and cope better with heat and dirt over long sessions.

63
00:04:48,648 --> 00:04:51,946
Ceramic is not automatically faster on the day.

64
00:04:51,946 --> 00:04:56,227
It can stay smooth for longer, but only if you look after it.

65
00:04:56,927 --> 00:04:59,102
Here is the inside of one wheel.

66
00:04:59,102 --> 00:05:01,209
The axle runs straight through.

67
00:05:01,209 --> 00:05:05,286
A bearing sits on each side, and between them is the spacer.

68
00:05:05,286 --> 00:05:12,286
The spacer carries the clamping load, so you can tighten the axle firmly without pinching the bearings.

69
00:05:12,286 --> 00:05:19,014
Skip it, and you are choosing between a loose wheel and a squeezed bearing, and neither rolls well.

70
00:05:19,014 --> 00:05:20,577
Bearings also need oil.

71
00:05:20,577 --> 00:05:27,033
A speed bearing wants a thin oil, not a thick grease, and one small drop per bearing is plenty.

72
00:05:27,033 --> 00:05:29,004
Extra oil only collects dust.

73
00:05:29,004 --> 00:05:34,441
Regular cleaning and a fresh drop keep a good bearing fast for a very long time.

74
00:05:34,441 --> 00:05:36,615
Neglect kills bearings, not age.

75
00:05:37,315 --> 00:05:41,027
At speed, most of the effort you fight is the air itself.

76
00:05:41,027 --> 00:05:45,520
A skin suit is a close fitting one piece made from compression lycra.

77
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:51,185
It cuts the drag of loose clothing and holds your muscles firmly through a long effort.

78
00:05:51,185 --> 00:05:52,617
It is not about looks.

79
00:05:52,617 --> 00:05:58,543
On a fast lap, the difference between a flapping shirt and a clean suit is real free speed.

80
00:05:58,543 --> 00:06:02,970
You do not need one to start racing, but many skaters add one early.

81
00:06:02,970 --> 00:06:08,570
Get the fit snug, because a loose suit gives back the very drag it is meant to remove.

82
00:06:09,270 --> 00:06:11,991
Now, the order to build your first setup.

83
00:06:11,991 --> 00:06:13,052
First, the boot.

84
00:06:13,052 --> 00:06:19,289
It should fit the length and width of your foot, lock your heel down, and not crush your toes.

85
00:06:19,289 --> 00:06:23,071
A poor boot hides every good part you bolt underneath it.

86
00:06:23,071 --> 00:06:30,834
Second, a four wheel frame with 90 or 100 millimetre wheels, to keep you low and stable while your technique arrives.

87
00:06:30,834 --> 00:06:38,598
Choose a firmness for your surface, because grip you can trust builds confidence faster than roll you cannot control.

88
00:06:38,598 --> 00:06:43,176
Third, a solid steel bearing set, correct spacers and thin skate oil.

89
00:06:43,176 --> 00:06:46,427
Learn to strip, clean and re oil a bearing early.

90
00:06:46,427 --> 00:06:51,403
Finally, when you upgrade, change one part and skate it for a few sessions.

91
00:06:51,403 --> 00:06:56,446
Change everything at once, and you will never know what made the difference.

92
00:06:57,146 --> 00:06:59,755
If you remember one thing, make it this.

93
00:06:59,755 --> 00:07:06,539
Start stable, keep your bearings clean, match your bore, and let your gear grow as your body catches up.

94
00:07:06,539 --> 00:07:12,410
Once the jargon stops getting in the way, you can make clear choices about your own setup.

95
00:07:12,410 --> 00:07:17,955
The full guide, with a glossary of every word in this video, is on the LiveLica blog.

96
00:07:17,955 --> 00:07:19,977
The link is in the description.
